#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:1
msgid "Release 2.9 (2022-05-30)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:3
msgid ""
"Running Nix with the new `--debugger` flag will cause it to start a repl "
"session if an exception is thrown during evaluation, or if `builtins.break` "
"is called.  From there you can inspect the values of variables and evaluate "
"Nix expressions.  In debug mode, the following new repl commands are "
"available:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:18
msgid "Read more about the debugger [here](https://www.zknotes.com/note/5970)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:21
msgid ""
"Nix now provides better integration with zsh's `run-help` feature. It is now "
"included in the Nix installation in the form of an autoloadable shell "
"function, `run-help-nix`. It picks up Nix subcommands from the currently "
"typed in command and directs the user to the associated man pages."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:27
msgid ""
"`nix repl` has a new build-and-link (`:bl`) command that builds a derivation "
"while creating GC root symlinks."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:30
msgid ""
"The path produced by `builtins.toFile` is now allowed to be imported or read "
"even with restricted evaluation. Note that this will not work with a read-"
"only store."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:34
msgid ""
"`nix build` has a new `--print-out-paths` flag to print the resulting output "
"paths.  This matches the default behaviour of `nix-build`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:38
msgid ""
"You can now specify which outputs of a derivation `nix` should operate on "
"using the syntax `installable^outputs`, e.g. `nixpkgs#glibc^dev,static` or "
"`nixpkgs#glibc^*`. By default, `nix` will use the outputs specified by the "
"derivation's `meta.outputsToInstall` attribute if it exists, or all outputs "
"otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-2.9.md:45
msgid ""
"`builtins.fetchTree` (and flake inputs) can now be used to fetch plain files "
"over the `http(s)` and `file` protocols in addition to directory tarballs."
msgstr ""
